WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.976
In 2005, investors were euphoric about the booming housing market. 

00:00:03.976 --> 00:00:08.733
Financial institutions raked in billions of dollars financing real estate ventures.

00:00:08.733 --> 00:00:11.541
Riding the wave of financial success.  

00:00:11.541 --> 00:00:15.657
But then, the wave broke, and it all came crashing down. 

00:00:15.657 --> 00:00:18.840
1 in 10 Americans couldn’t afford their mortgage.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.760
Nearly 10 million Americans&nbsp;
lost their homes to foreclosure.

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:29.400
Housing prices fell 30% from their highs, and in&nbsp;
big cities like Miami, they dropped around 40%.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:33.480
The result was the worst global economic&nbsp;
crisis since the Great Depression.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:37.320
Yet despite the disaster, one trader triumphed.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:40.800
John Paulson predicted the&nbsp;
housing market would collapse.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.520
He bet against it even when&nbsp;
other investors laughed him off.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:48.600
And here’s the thing - Paulson didn’t&nbsp;
know a thing about real estate.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.400
He was an expert in mergers and acquisitions.

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:55.400
Yet during the market meltdown, when&nbsp;
other firms lost billions of dollars,

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:58.800
he made $20 billion for&nbsp;
his firm and his clients.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.160
That's why it's called the greatest trade ever.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:06.520
This is the story of how an outsider&nbsp;
pulled off the greatest trade of all time.

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:11.720
Before the crisis unfolded, John&nbsp;
Paulson was a small fish in a big pond.

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:17.760
In 2005, his funds only gained 5%, below&nbsp;
the average of 9% at other hedge funds.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:22.960
At age 49, he was also older than many of&nbsp;
his peers who were in their 20s and 30s.

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:26.400
He had catching up to do because&nbsp;
after attending NYU and Harvard

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:29.400
Business School, he went into consulting first.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:33.920
But he soon realized he’d rather make money for&nbsp;
himself than advise others on how to make money.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:37.640
So, he got a job at investment bank&nbsp;
Bear Stearns and then Gruss where he

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:41.600
gained valuable experience and&nbsp;
also partied hard after hours.

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:45.880
Despite rising through the ranks, he sensed&nbsp;
that greater opportunities lay ahead.

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:49.720
In 1994, he started his own&nbsp;
hedge fund, Paulson &amp; Co.

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:53.640
Trouble is, the phone didn’t ring.&nbsp;
No one wanted to invest with him.

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:58.760
As Paulson recounted to Gregory Zuckerman in his&nbsp;
book, “The Greatest Trade Ever, “It was hard to

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:05.120
be rejected; it was a lonely period. After a while&nbsp;
I said, this is too much. He lifted my spirits.”

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:06.680
Referring to his dad.

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:11.400
Paulson also clung to Winston Churchill’s&nbsp;
famous “never give up” commencement speech.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:15.560
Since no one took a chance on him, he&nbsp;
decided to take a chance on himself.

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:19.000
He started his firm with $2&nbsp;
million of his own money.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.840
It took him a full year to find his first client.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:24.920
He barely had enough resources to hire employees,

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:29.440
though he did hire an assistant, Jenny&nbsp;
Zaharia, who would later become his wife.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:32.840
In the 1990s, his firm managed&nbsp;
tens of millions of dollars,

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:36.240
whereas the big hedge funds&nbsp;
managed hundreds of millions.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:40.240
But the events of nine eleven would&nbsp;
change the trajectory of his firm.

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:42.600
In response to the shock and uncertainty,

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:46.440
the Federal Reserve cut interest&nbsp;
rates to try to stimulate the economy.

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:51.480
This made borrowing cheaper, including for&nbsp;
mortgages, allowing more people to afford homes.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:55.680
Yet this inadvertently laid the&nbsp;
groundwork for the housing crisis.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:59.080
Banks and other mortgage lenders started&nbsp;
loosening their standards, offering

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:04.360
mortgages even to those with poor credit.
 
“If you were a borrower and you had no credit,

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:09.880
you had a horrible credit rating, you had&nbsp;
a history of never paying your money back

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:19.480
and you had no cash to put down to buy the&nbsp;
house, they would still lend you 110% of

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:23.880
the purchase price of the house. So you&nbsp;
can actually buy the house with no money

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:30.760
down and get 10% of the purchase price in&nbsp;
your pocket, as crazy as that may sound.

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:38.720
By 2005, 24% of all mortgages required&nbsp;
no down payment, up from 3% in 2001.

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:41.600
These risky loans, known as subprime mortgages,

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:44.840
were appealing to lenders due&nbsp;
to their higher interest rates.

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:49.400
Banks packaged these subprime mortgages into&nbsp;
bundles and sold them to investment banks like

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:54.360
Lehman Brothers and Merrill Lynch which converted&nbsp;
these bundles into mortgage-backed securities,

00:03:54.360 --> 00:04:00.000
selling them to a range of investors worldwide,&nbsp;
from Japanese pension funds to Swiss banks.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.480
When an increasing number of homeowners&nbsp;
couldn’t afford their mortgage payments,

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:07.280
leading to widespread defaults, it&nbsp;
triggered a domino effect through

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:10.680
the financial system, which&nbsp;
experts failed to foresee.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:17.440
According to Moody’s and S&amp;P, there had&nbsp;
never been a default of an investment-grade

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:25.720
mortgage-backed security. Never being I guess&nbsp;
post-WWII or post-1950. And we said, ‘Oh well,

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:30.000
they did default in the Great Depression.’&nbsp;
They said, ‘Well, unless you think we’re gonna

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.440
have a Great Depression, we don’t think mortgage&nbsp;
securities are gonna default.’ And they laughed.

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:37.440
But Paulson didn’t think the past would predict

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:40.760
the future given the risky&nbsp;
mortgages being issued.

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:45.880
The only thing that kept these mortgages afloat&nbsp;
was that housing prices were rising rapidly.

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:48.240
If you couldn’t pay your mortgage and defaulted,

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:53.200
the lender would take back the house, but would&nbsp;
still profit as the property value went up.

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:56.800
But what would happen if&nbsp;
housing prices started to fall?

00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:59.080
Paolo Pellegrini wondered the same.

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:02.320
He had asked Paulson for a job&nbsp;
after his career had derailed,

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:04.880
convinced that his Italian accent had hurt him.

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:10.040
Pellegrini poured over mountains of&nbsp;
housing data and noticed a troubling trend:

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:17.000
Home prices had increased an average of 1.4%&nbsp;
between 1975 to 2000 factoring in inflation

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:23.760
but afterward, soared 7% a year on average&nbsp;
from 2000 to 2005, hinting at a bubble.

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:27.360
His analysis also showed that when&nbsp;
housing prices fell in the past,

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:33.160
they dropped below the trend line, suggesting when&nbsp;
the housing market corrected, it would be brutal.

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:36.960
He and Paulson viewed the housing&nbsp;
market as a house of cards,

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:40.040
bound to collapse when housing prices fell.

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:45.520
It was kind of a very simple physics problem. If&nbsp;
home prices kept falling and went negative and

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:51.320
delinquencies kept rising, at some point the&nbsp;
mortgage market would collapse and then these

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:59.240
mortgages would sustain losses. It became very&nbsp;
obvious to us but the problem is, the machinery,

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:05.000
the factories that were just geared up to&nbsp;
buy mortgages, securitizing and selling them,

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:10.600
they just were oblivious to what was&nbsp;
going on. They couldn’t stop the machine.”

00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:16.080
Pellegrini proposed that they bet against risky&nbsp;
mortgages by purchasing credit-default swaps.

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:21.680
Credit-default swaps - or CDS - acted like&nbsp;
insurance on mortgage-backed securities.

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:24.320
It’s like buying insurance for a house.

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:28.640
If the house catches on fire (which can be&nbsp;
compared to default on the mortgage-backed

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:34.360
security), the insurance company, or the seller&nbsp;
of the credit-default swap, compensates the buyer.

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:38.720
The cost of insurance was only around&nbsp;
1% of the amount being protected.

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:45.000
For example, it cost $10 million annually to&nbsp;
ensure $1 billion of mortgage-backed securities.

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:47.880
If their bet didn’t go well,&nbsp;
they’d lose $10 million.

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:51.400
But if the securities defaulted,&nbsp;
they’d get $1 billion.

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:58.920
In essence we risked 1% in order to make&nbsp;
100% if they defaulted within the average

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:05.520
timeline of these CDS which was about&nbsp;
two years. So it was a very skewed trade

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:11.160
where you lost little if the trade didn’t&nbsp;
work but you made a lot if it did work.

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:14.720
Understanding credit-default swaps&nbsp;
required specialized knowledge.

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:17.240
Pellegrini educated himself through tutorials

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:21.320
provided by Bear Stearns brokers&nbsp;
and also at industry conferences.

00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:25.360
Their strategy involved targeting the&nbsp;
riskier mortgage-backed securities.

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:30.680
Mortgage-backed securities are divided into&nbsp;
different layers or slices, known as tranches.

00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:33.160
Each tranche has a different level of risk.

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:38.280
The top tranches like AAA are considered&nbsp;
the safest, the bottom ones the riskiest.

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:43.600
Paulson strategically targeted BBB slices,&nbsp;
believing that a housing collapse would impact

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:49.280
BBB-rated tranches more than the “safer” A ones. 
 
His firm bought $25 billion worth of

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:52.000
credit-default swaps with all the major banks.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:56.800
The total value insured was substantial&nbsp;
even with only a 1% annual premium.

00:07:56.800 --> 00:07:59.560
Paulson established a new&nbsp;
fund dedicated to betting

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:02.680
against subprime mortgages on a large scale.

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:06.920
However, finding investors was difficult&nbsp;
because few believed in his strategy.

00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:10.280
As described in Zuckerman’s book,&nbsp;
Jeffrey Tarrant of the hedge fund

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:14.040
Protégé Partners said, “People on&nbsp;
trading desks warned, ‘He (Paulson)

00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:18.040
doesn’t have expertise in the area,’&nbsp;
or ‘He doesn’t know what he’s doing’.”

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:20.320
A Bear Sterns expert said: “You guys need to

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:23.520
do more research on historical&nbsp;
price appreciation.” - page 155

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:26.400
When Paulson asked, “What&nbsp;
are your models based on?”

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:31.560
The expert replied: “Our models are fine.&nbsp;
We’ve been doing this for 20 years.”

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:35.360
Paulson found himself bewildered by&nbsp;
the lack of interest in his fund.

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:40.920
He lamented, “I don’t know why they don’t&nbsp;
get it…this is the trade of a lifetime.”

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:46.720
He ended up scrounging together $147 million&nbsp;
for his new fund, mostly from friends.

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:48.920
There were other traders who foresaw a collapse,

00:08:48.920 --> 00:08:53.640
but they weren’t as determined as&nbsp;
Paulson and didn’t invest nearly as much.

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:58.800
Unlike Paulson, Michael Burry failed to convince&nbsp;
investors to support a dedicated fund for betting

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:04.040
against subprime mortgages. When investors started&nbsp;
withdrawing money from his firm, he reluctantly

00:09:04.040 --> 00:09:09.720
began to sell his credit-default swaps. Despite&nbsp;
scoring half a billion in gains for his firm,

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:14.680
he wondered what might have been if he hadn't&nbsp;
been forced to sell some of his positions early.

00:09:14.680 --> 00:09:19.080
Andrew Lahde’s boss was furious at his&nbsp;
bearish housing bets and fired him.

00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:23.080
So he set up his own hedge fund and&nbsp;
earned $100 million for his clients,

00:09:23.080 --> 00:09:24.840
and $10 million for himself.

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:27.640
When he retired, he wrote a&nbsp;
letter sticking it to those

00:09:27.640 --> 00:09:30.360
who failed to see the warning signs of a crash:

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:32.000
"I was in this for the money.

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:36.120
The low-hanging fruit, i.e., idiots&nbsp;
whose parents paid for prep school,

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:39.920
Yale, and then the Harvard&nbsp;
MBA, was there for the taking."

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:44.160
Greg Lippmann at Deutsche Bank played a&nbsp;
crucial role in facilitating credit-default

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:49.000
swap contracts for Paulson. Lippmann's&nbsp;
own bets against housing were successful,

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:53.560
but the troubles at his bank meant he&nbsp;
was only given a few million as a bonus.

00:09:53.560 --> 00:09:57.080
Paulson had encouraged his friend,&nbsp;
real-estate millionaire Jeffrey Greene

00:09:57.080 --> 00:10:02.400
to invest in his fund, but Greene decided to&nbsp;
secure credit-default swap contracts on his own,

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:05.707
which Paulson viewed as a stab in&nbsp;
the back.

00:10:05.707 --> 00:10:09.080
Greene pocketed $500 million on his bets.

00:10:09.080 --> 00:10:11.600
Although all of these investors 
walked away very wealthy,

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:14.760
none matched the scale of Paulson’s earnings.

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:18.960
Paulson went all in, certain&nbsp;
everything would collapse.

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:23.200
In addition to shorting mortgage-backed&nbsp;
securities, his team also shorted banks

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:27.640
that had large quantities of subprime&nbsp;
mortgages: New Century, Fannie Mae,

00:10:27.640 --> 00:10:33.400
Freddie Mac, Citibank, Washington Mutual,&nbsp;
IndyMac, Bear Stearns, Lehman Brothers.

00:10:33.400 --> 00:10:37.000
Paulson’s top trader, Brad&nbsp;
Rosenberg, summed it up this way:

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:41.240
“We had to get on as many trades as&nbsp;
possible before it was too late.”

00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:43.400
The wait, however, seemed endless.

00:10:43.400 --> 00:10:46.600
By late 2006, the housing market was slowing down,

00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:50.520
yet the widespread mortgage defaults&nbsp;
they anticipated hadn’t yet occurred.

00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:54.440
The main obstacle was the role of&nbsp;
Collateralized Debt Obligations (CDOs).

00:10:54.440 --> 00:10:57.920
These complex financial products&nbsp;
which bundled various types of debt

00:10:57.920 --> 00:11:02.240
including mortgage-backed securities&nbsp;
masked the true risks in the market.

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:05.960
They were like the sturdier lower&nbsp;
blocks of a tower about to fall over,

00:11:05.960 --> 00:11:08.280
delaying the inevitable collapse.

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:15.480
Paulson reassured his concerned wife, Jenny,&nbsp;
saying, “It’s just a matter of waiting.”

00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:21.560
The collapse began in late 2006 when New Century,&nbsp;
one of the largest subprime mortgage lenders,

00:11:21.560 --> 00:11:25.920
reported an unexpected loss as its&nbsp;
borrowers struggled to pay their loans.

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:30.800
In 2007, two big hedge funds run by Bear&nbsp;
Sterns that were heavily invested in

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:35.760
subprime mortgage-backed securities&nbsp;
imploded, triggering market chaos.

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:39.320
The heads of Citigroup and&nbsp;
Merril Lynch were ousted.

00:11:39.320 --> 00:11:43.280
And then, in September 2008,&nbsp;
Lehman Brothers went under,

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:49.320
the largest bankruptcy in U.S. history that&nbsp;
sent shockwaves through the financial system.

00:11:49.320 --> 00:11:53.200
The U.S. government stepped in to rescue&nbsp;
the government-sponsored enterprises,

00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:57.240
Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac,&nbsp;
guaranteeing over $300 billion

00:11:57.240 --> 00:12:01.440
of debt, motivated by a desire&nbsp;
to stabilize the housing market.

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:05.040
The fallout from the financial&nbsp;
crisis was devastating:

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:11.680
10 million American homeowners faced foreclosure,&nbsp;
unemployment soared, and retirement savings and

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:16.360
investments plummeted as the stock market&nbsp;
fell 60%. (from fall 2007 to early 2009)

00:12:16.360 --> 00:12:20.680
And yet, in the turmoil, Paulson&nbsp;
saw an unprecedented opportunity,

00:12:20.680 --> 00:12:22.440
and it changed his life.

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:29.520
Paulson &amp; Co. earned $15 billion in 2007,&nbsp;
with Paulson himself making $4 billion. Over

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:35.560
the next two years, his firm added another $5&nbsp;
billion, with Paulson taking home $2 billion.

00:12:35.560 --> 00:12:40.400
He was no longer a mediocre&nbsp;
player but a Wall Street legend.

00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:48.640
Paolo Pellegrini, who was often overshadowed by&nbsp;
Paulson, was rewarded with $175 million in 2007.

00:12:48.640 --> 00:12:52.440
The possibility that we’re in another&nbsp;
housing bubble is a topic of debate.

00:12:52.440 --> 00:12:56.960
The financial crisis led to significant&nbsp;
regulatory changes like stricter lending

00:12:56.960 --> 00:13:00.000
standards to try to avert another disaster.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:03.680
Yet, despite these measures,&nbsp;
future bubbles can’t be ruled out.

00:13:03.680 --> 00:13:07.600
Financial markets are complex and&nbsp;
influenced by a wide range of factors,

00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:12.440
including economic policies, market&nbsp;
conditions, and investor behavior.

00:13:12.440 --> 00:13:15.240
John Paulson’s story is a powerful reminder of the

00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:20.760
value of in-depth analysis and independent&nbsp;
thinking, even when few believed him.

00:13:20.760 --> 00:13:25.400
Sometimes, the most insightful decisions&nbsp;
are those that go against the grain.

